W("Extract-based: 'Bholi felt as if suddenly all the bells in the village temple were ringing and the trees in front of the school-house had blossomed into big red flowers. Her heart was throbbing with a new hope and a new life.' (i) What triggered this? (ii) What is the figure of speech? (iii) What does it show about Bholi? (iv) What is the mood?", 4, "(i) The teacher's promise that Bholi would learn to read and speak and be respected. (1) (ii) Imagery and metaphor/hyperbole (bells ringing, trees blossoming). (1) (iii) She had never been given hope, so this joy is overwhelming. (1) (iv) Joyful and hopeful. (1)", "H"),
W("Extract-based: 'Ramlal went and placed his turban - his honour - at Bishamber's feet. \"Do not humiliate me so. Take two thousand rupees.\"' (i) What does the turban stand for? (ii) What does his action show? (iii) How does Bishamber reply? (iv) What does Ramlal do next?", 4, "(i) His honour (izzat). (1) (ii) Desperation and humiliation; he sacrifices his pride to save his family's name. (1) (iii) He refuses: 'No. Five thousand, or we go back. Keep your daughter.' (1) (iv) Tears streaming, he opens the safe and places the notes at the bridegroom's feet. (1)", "H"),
W("'Bholi's teacher played an important role in changing the course of her life.' Explain in about 100 words.", 5, "The teacher was the first person to treat Bholi kindly. Her soft voice and patience helped Bholi to say her name, and she explained that fear caused the stammer. She promised that Bholi would learn to read and be respected, and gave her a picture book. Over the years she gave Bholi education and confidence, so that Bholi could speak out against injustice and plan her own life, even to become a teacher. She was Bholi's guide and the artist of her 'masterpiece'. (5; content 3, expression 2)", "H"),
W("'Why did Bholi at first agree to an unequal match? Why did she later reject the marriage? What does this tell us about her?' Answer in about 100 words.", 5, "At first Bholi agreed because she thought it her duty to save her parents' izzat; she believed no one would marry her. But when Bishamber demanded five thousand rupees after seeing her pock-marks, she realised that he was greedy and a coward and that her father was being humiliated. She flung away the garland and refused. This shows that she is self-respecting, brave and independent, and that education has given her confidence and a voice. (5; content 3, expression 2)", "H"),
W("'Bholi's real name is Sulekha, but only in the last-but-one paragraph is she called Sulekha again. Why?' Give your reasoning in about 80 words.", 4, "'Bholi' means a simpleton, a name given by others for her weakness. At the end she has become confident, educated and independent, so the author gives her her own name, Sulekha, which marks her new identity and dignity. Her calm, steady voice and clear plan for the future show she is no longer a simpleton. (4)", "H"),
W("'Do you think girl children are not treated at par with boys?' Answer with reference to the story and your own observation (about 100 words).", 5, "In the story the sons were sent to the city to study, while the daughters were considered a burden; Ramlal's wife said, 'If girls go to school, who will marry them?' Bholi was neglected and married off for convenience. In real life, though much has improved, some families still prefer boys in education and give girls fewer chances. Schemes such as Beti Bachao Beti Padhao aim to correct this. Girls and boys should have equal rights and duties. (5; content 3, expression 2)", "H"),
W("'Should girls be aware of their rights and assert them?' Give your view with two reasons (about 80 words).", 4, "Yes. Girls should know their rights to education, safety and choice, just as boys do. First, awareness gives them confidence to resist injustice, as Bholi refused a greedy marriage. Second, educated and aware girls can build a better future for their families and society. Rights must go with responsibilities for both boys and girls. (4)", "H"),
W("Do you think the characters spoke English? Give clues from the story.", 4, "No. The characters have Indian names (Ramlal, Bholi, Radha, Bishamber), and words such as Pitaji, izzat, numberdar, Tehsildar and Aunty are used. They would speak Hindi, Punjabi or a local language of north India, and the story is translated. (4)", "H"),
W("Case study: In a village near Ajmer, a girl called Pooja wants to continue studies after Class 8, but her family plans her marriage. Using the lesson, suggest three ways to help her.", 5, "A teacher can talk gently to her parents about the benefits of education, as the Tehsildar urged Ramlal; she can be told about the government scheme and scholarships; and relatives or villagers can support her as the teacher supported Bholi. Pooja herself can speak calmly about her wish. (5)", "H", wp=True),
W("Why do you think the author describes Bholi's life so fully before the school scene? What does it add?", 4, "It builds sympathy for her and shows how neglect and cruelty shaped her; the contrast with her later strength becomes bigger and more moving. It also criticises social attitudes: poverty is not the cause, since the household is prosperous, but prejudice against girls and against disability. (4)", "H"),
W("Write a diary entry (about 100 words) by Bholi on the night before her wedding, as she listens to her parents.", 5, "Model: I lay awake and heard Pitaji and Ma speaking about me. They say I am witless, a dumb cow, and that I should be lucky to get a rich grocer who is old and lame. I do not mind that he limps, but I minded their words. My teacher told me to put the fear out of my heart. I will not make trouble; I will do it for Pitaji's izzat. But I wonder what the man is like. Tomorrow I shall see. (5; content 2, expression 2, format 1)", "H"),
W("Write a letter (about 100 words) from Bholi, now a teacher, to her former teacher thanking her.", 5, "Model: Respected Ma'am, I am writing to thank you for all that you did for me. When I first came to school I could not say my name, but you told me to put the fear out of my heart. Today I teach in the same school where you taught me. My parents are old and I look after them. I tell my students that no one is a simpleton; each needs a kind word and a chance. I shall never forget that day in the classroom corner. With respect, Sulekha. (5; content 2, expression 2, format 1)", "H"),
