 ("table", ["Point", "Detail"], [["Genre", "Short story with a social message"], ["Setting", "A village that becomes a small town, in north India"], ["Main character", "Bholi (Sulekha), the youngest of seven children"], ["Mood", "Pitiful at first, hopeful in the school scene, triumphant at the end"], ["Theme", "Education, equality of girls, self-respect and courage"]]),
 ("h2", "2. Summary in order"),
 ("ol", [
  "Sulekha, called Bholi (simpleton), is the fourth daughter of Numberdar Ramlal. At ten months she fell from a cot on her head; at two she had smallpox, which left black pock-marks. She could not speak till five and stammered, so children mimicked her and she talked very little.",
  "Ramlal's prosperous household had three sons and four daughters. The sons studied in the city; Radha was married, Mangla's marriage was settled and Champa would be next. Ramlal was worried about Bholi, who had neither good looks nor intelligence.",
  "When Bholi was seven, a girls' primary school opened. The Tehsildar told Ramlal to send his daughters to school. His wife replied, 'If girls go to school, who will marry them?' but suggested sending Bholi, since she was unlikely to marry. Ramlal took her the next day.",
  "Bholi was frightened, remembering the old cow Lakshmi that had been sold. She was given a clean dress, bathed and oiled. At school she liked the pictures. The teacher spoke softly, helped her say her name and promised that she would learn to read and be respected. Bholi felt a new hope.",
  "Years passed; the village became a town. Ramlal and his wife decided to accept the proposal of Bishamber Nath, a rich, older, lame grocer who asked for no dowry. Bholi overheard them.",
  "At the wedding Bishamber lifted the garland, saw her pock-marks and demanded five thousand rupees. Ramlal placed his turban at his feet and offered two thousand; finally he paid five thousand. Bishamber smiled with triumph.",
  "When he asked for the garland, Bholi looked at him with cold contempt, flung the garland in the fire and said in a clear voice, 'Pitaji! Take back your money. I am not going to marry this man.' She called him greedy, mean and a coward.",
  "Bishamber left. Ramlal, ashamed, asked what would become of her. Bholi calmly promised to serve her parents and to teach in the same school. The teacher watched with the satisfaction of an artist who has finished a masterpiece."]),
