 ("h2", "7. Extract explanations"),
 ("p", "**Extract 1:** 'The teacher's voice was so soft and soothing! In all her life she had never been called like that. It touched her heart.' For the first time Bholi is addressed with kindness. This small act wins her trust and starts her transformation."),
 ("p", "**Extract 2:** 'Her eyes were not downcast. She was looking up, looking straight at her prospective husband, and in her eyes there was neither anger nor hate, only cold contempt.' Bholi's posture shows her change from submission to dignity. Contempt is a calm, controlled feeling, not a burst of rage."),
 ("p", "**Extract 3:** 'Now the dumb cow, the stammering fool, is speaking. Do you want to hear more?' Bholi turns the insults of her family on her accusers. This is the high point of the story, showing that education has given her the power of speech."),
 ("p", "**Extract 4:** 'For the sake of your izzat, I was willing to marry this lame old man. But I will not have such a mean, greedy and contemptible coward as my husband.' Bholi explains her two decisions at once. She had been ready to sacrifice her own wishes for her father's honour, but when the same honour was used to extort money from him, she saw that obeying would be wrong. The three adjectives, 'mean, greedy and contemptible', are placed in order, each stronger than the last, so the speech grows in force."),
